Common Questions About GEO

What is Generative Engine Optimization (GEO), and how is it different from traditional SEO?

Generative Engine Optimization (GEO) focuses on helping brands show up in AI-driven outputs, and the key differences from traditional SEO come down to where visibility happens and how [success is measured](https://www.perrill.com/what-metrics-should-i-use-to-measure-success/). Traditional SEO focuses on improving performance in organic search results and search rankings, while GEO is built to influence ai-generated answers. Unlike standard SEO, GEO involves creating content, building mentions, and utilizing strategies that align with how generative engines interpret meaning, context, and user intent. GEO performance depends more on citations and inclusion in AI responses than on traditional keyword rankings alone. This means going beyond meta tags and link building to shape your brand’s entire digital presence in a more conversational, AI-friendly way. By combining data-driven insights with creative storytelling, GEO bridges the gap between human-centric messaging and machine-generated understanding.



Why do I need GEO now?

Generative engines are redefining how people search, discover, and engage with online content. When an AI Overview appears, webpages can see a 34.5 percent lower average click-through rate than similar searches without an ai generated summary. As more platforms adopt advanced AI, securing top visibility requires a proactive approach that speaks the language of these engines. Waiting too long to adapt can mean missing out on valuable leads and conversions. With GEO, you’ll be ahead of the curve—[positioning your brand](https://www.perrill.com/what-is-brand-positioning/) as an authoritative source that AI-driven tools trust. Your customers are already using LLMs and AI chatbots to find services similar to yours. If you aren’t showing up, then you are losing customers.



