Do I need both SEO and GEO?

As with anything related to marketing, it depends. Many users are shifting to an AI-search first mentality. If your target audience is part of this shift, then your broader strategy should cover both traditional search and generative AI, with organic search results on one side and AI discovery on the other. Fortunately, this doesn’t mean you have to duplicate efforts. A lot of what works to rank better on Google will help you be more visible in ChatGPT, for example. That doesn’t mean you will automatically see results in AI search. We recommend dedicating part of your marketing budget to Google/Bing optimization and another part to ChatGPT/Perplexity/Claude optimization.



Isn’t GEO just doing good SEO?

This isn’t a wrong take, but it’s an oversimplified take. Most seo focuses on improving visibility in familiar search channels, and traditional seo focuses on getting your website to rank better on Google for target keywords, while GEO also prepares brands for answer engines and ai-generated responses. That’s not how AI chatbots work. There aren’t keywords to optimize for, at least not in the same way there are keywords on Google. The efforts you are doing for SEO right now should help with generative engine optimization as well, but they won’t get you all the way there. To be more visible on AI search engines, you need more mentions, different content, positive online sentiment, and more. Brands should also track branded search trends, engagement, and pipeline impact alongside ai mentions and citation data instead of relying only on traditional search metrics. Yes, those are all components of SEO and marketing in general, but they need to be dedicated to the specific platforms. Ranking on Google doesn’t mean you will show up on ChatGPT (although the correlation is pretty high).

